w lines  Add ONE test whose subject is a failure. Any of these satisfies it:  ```rust #[test] fn edge_a_bad_input_is_refused_rather_than_panicking() {     let r = run_source("set x to list.map(5, to_text)");     assert!(r.is_err(), "a non-list receiver must be a clean error"); } ```  ```rust #[test] fn edge_the_vm_and_the_tree_walker_agree_on_a_failure() {     assert!(run_on_treewalker(BAD_PROGRAM).is_err());     assert!(run_on_bytecode(BAD_PROGRAM).is_err()); } ```  Or, if a case genuinely must panic rather than return an error:  ```rust #[test] #[should_panic] fn edge_out_of_range_index_is_caught() { /* ... */ } ```  Pick the input that this phase's change makes reachable and that the language promises is an error. **It must be a case you have actually verified produces an error** — do not write `assert!(true)` and do not assert on a case you have not run.  ## The rule, stated once  > Every test asserts something that can fail. A test that only ever succeeds is > a smoke test. The gate counts tests that assert a **failure was produced**.  `AGENTS.md` §3.1 calls this out separately from the quota and from the `edge_*` requirement, precisely because it is the easiest of the four to satisfy by accident.  ## Do not redo the rest  The re-measurement in your REPORT is correct and worth keeping. Quota is met, `edge_*` is met, the code is green. **Add the one test, re-run, report.**  Budget ten minutes, not ninety. You do not need to re-derive anything. ```
